    "case_id": "590",
    "case_summary": "A 69-year-old man with a history of untreated hypertension presented with intermittent chest pain over 10 days and heavy chest pain lasting for 3 hours in the previous 5 days. The time onset from symptoms to medical attention was approximately 5 days.\n\nAt initial presentation:- ECG showed abnormal Q wave and ST-segment elevation with terminal T-wave inversion in leads II, III, and aVF- Small pericardial effusion notedAt 6-month follow-up:- Blood pressure 123/79 mmHg- Heart rate 74 beats/min- No cardiac murmur- No signs of congestion- ECG showed abnormal Q wave, mildly elevated ST segment, and negative T waves in leads II, III, and aVF\n\nInitial Tests:- Laboratory Studies:  * Mildly elevated cardiac enzymes (creatine kinase: 237 U/L)  * High-sensitivity troponin I: 3348 pg/mL  * C-reactive protein: 3.8 mg/dL- Imaging Studies:  1. Transthoracic Echocardiography:     * Showed inferior-posterior left ventricular wall akinesis with small pericardial effusion     Figure 1 (A,B): Short-axis view showing wall abnormalities  2. Contrast-enhanced CT:     * Pericardial effusion with CT value of 40 Hounsfield units     * No aortic dissection     Figure 1 (C): CT imaging findings  3. Coronary Angiography:     * Thrombotic obstruction in right coronary artery     * Significant stenosis in left anterior descending and circumflex arteries     Figure 1 (D,E): Right coronary angiograms6-Month Follow-up Tests:  1. Echocardiography:     * Large aneurysm formation with myocardial defect (3 cm diameter) in inferior wall     Figure 2 (A,B): Short-axis view showing aneurysm  2. Cardiac CT Angiography:     * Neck diameter: 14 × 20 mm     * Maximum diameter: 29 × 31 mm     * Free wall sealed by wall-adherent pericardium     * No adherent thrombosis     Figure 3 (A-C): CT angiography of left ventricle",
